{
  "abstract": "A separated seven-color inequality carrier is a seven-colorable graph with two nonadjacent terminals that differ in every seven-coloring, whose join with two adjacent universal apices has planar layers separating the terminals after the apices are deleted. We prove that every private vertex in such a carrier has two adjacent private nonneighbors: the private complement has no simplicial vertex. Coverage may be partial, and the carrier need not be minimal. Every carrier has at least thirteen private vertices; at that order its private graph is seven-vertex-critical with 41–43 edges and full terminal coverage. The first ingredient is a marked critical-graph density theorem: if $J$ is seven-vertex-critical and $J-z$ is $K_6$-free, then $e(J)\\geq3|V(J)|+4$, with only two equality types. New bounds at orders thirteen through sixteen are 44,47,50,53; the order-thirteen argument uses 4,185 checked positive colorings. We certify all 804 remaining Ore geometric cases with independently reconstructed necessary formulas, complete coverage, actual drawing controls, and full and trimmed RUP replay. An alternative verification supplies the prior classical premise. Overlapping critical cores and independent covered proxies handle uncovered vertices while preserving the coloring disjunction. A separate hand theorem excludes every fully covered fresh-module replacement of $K_8$ with two shared universal apices, without assuming separated drawings in advance. A triangulated-layer routing argument and a connected-deletion face bound explain the global obstruction. These results constrain both local private structure and a complete assembly mechanism; they do not resolve the Earth–Moon problem. A compact source supplement reproduces the finite evidence using four CPU processes.",
